WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.569
Lesson 4 becomes much clearer when a transformer is treated as linked induction through a shared core instead of as a ratio rule that appears without a mechanism.

2
00:00:05.569 --> 00:00:11.138
Primary coil means source of changing flux.

3
00:00:11.138 --> 00:00:16.707
An alternating current in the primary makes the magnetic state of the core keep changing.

4
00:00:16.707 --> 00:00:22.276
Core means shared magnetic bridge.

5
00:00:22.276 --> 00:00:27.844
The changing flux links both coils through the same magnetic pathway.

6
00:00:27.844 --> 00:00:33.413
Secondary coil means receives induced emf.

7
00:00:33.413 --> 00:00:38.982
A changing flux linkage in the secondary induces a voltage there too.

8
00:00:38.982 --> 00:00:44.551
Transformer action is an induction story built on shared changing core flux.

9
00:00:44.551 --> 00:00:50.120
Keep this takeaway in view: Transformer action is an induction story built on shared changing core flux.

10
00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:55.553
This diagram matters because it keeps the ratio relations attached to the changing-flux bridge that causes them.

11
00:00:55.553 --> 00:01:00.987
Input side means alternating current.

12
00:01:00.987 --> 00:01:06.420
The primary must have changing current for the core flux to change.

13
00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:11.853
Bridge means shared flux.

14
00:01:11.853 --> 00:01:17.287
The core carries the same changing magnetic story across the two coils.

15
00:01:17.287 --> 00:01:22.720
Output side means induced voltage.

16
00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:28.153
The secondary voltage depends on how many turns are linked to that changing flux.

17
00:01:28.153 --> 00:01:33.587
The ratio rules only make sense after the shared-flux bridge is visible.

18
00:01:33.587 --> 00:01:39.020
Keep this takeaway in view: The ratio rules only make sense after the shared-flux bridge is visible.

19
00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:44.007
A shared-medium analogy helps because the coils do not have to be directly connected electrically.

20
00:01:44.007 --> 00:01:48.994
They are linked through the magnetic changes passing through the core.

21
00:01:48.994 --> 00:01:53.981
Driver means primary coil.

22
00:01:53.981 --> 00:01:58.968
This side creates the changing magnetic disturbance.

23
00:01:58.968 --> 00:02:03.955
Bridge means core.

24
00:02:03.955 --> 00:02:08.942
The changing magnetic pattern is carried through the shared core.

25
00:02:08.942 --> 00:02:13.929
Receiver means secondary coil.

26
00:02:13.929 --> 00:02:18.916
This side responds because the same changing flux links its turns.

27
00:02:18.916 --> 00:02:23.903
The transformer works because both coils share one changing magnetic bridge.

28
00:02:23.903 --> 00:02:28.890
Keep this takeaway in view: The transformer works because both coils share one changing magnetic bridge.

29
00:02:28.890 --> 00:02:34.728
Lesson 4 is strongest when the learner first uses the turns ratio for voltage and then immediately uses ideal power balance to make sense of the current trade-off.

30
00:02:34.728 --> 00:02:40.565
Voltage job means turns ratio.

31
00:02:40.565 --> 00:02:46.402
More turns on one side means larger induced voltage on that side.

32
00:02:46.402 --> 00:02:52.240
Current job means power balance.

33
00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:58.078
If voltage rises ideally, current falls to keep power matched.

34
00:02:58.078 --> 00:03:03.915
Lesson gain means step-up and step-down become quantitative.

35
00:03:03.915 --> 00:03:09.752
The labels only matter because the ratios make measurable predictions.

36
00:03:09.752 --> 00:03:15.590
Keep these relations visible: V_p / V_s = N_p / N_s; V_p I_p = V_s I_s; and I_p / I_s = N_s / N_p.

37
00:03:15.590 --> 00:03:21.427
Do not stop after the voltage ratio.

38
00:03:21.427 --> 00:03:27.265
Ideal transformer questions often need the current bridge as well.

39
00:03:27.265 --> 00:03:33.102
Use turns for voltage and power balance for current.

40
00:03:33.102 --> 00:03:38.940
Keep this takeaway in view: Use turns for voltage and power balance for current.

41
00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:43.645
A dependable Lesson 4 route begins with the changing primary current, then follows the shared core flux, then uses the turns ratio for voltage and the power bridge for current.

42
00:03:43.645 --> 00:03:48.351
Step 1 means changing primary current.

43
00:03:48.351 --> 00:03:53.056
No changing current means no changing flux bridge.

44
00:03:53.056 --> 00:03:57.762
Step 2 means shared core flux.

45
00:03:57.762 --> 00:04:02.467
Both coils are linked by the same changing magnetic story.

46
00:04:02.467 --> 00:04:07.173
Step 3 means turns ratio.

47
00:04:07.173 --> 00:04:11.878
Use turns to relate the voltages.

48
00:04:11.878 --> 00:04:16.584
Step 4 means power-current link.

49
00:04:16.584 --> 00:04:21.289
Use ideal power balance to infer the current change.

50
00:04:21.289 --> 00:04:25.995
Transformer questions are easiest when mechanism and ratios are kept in sequence.

51
00:04:25.995 --> 00:04:30.700
Keep this takeaway in view: Transformer questions are easiest when mechanism and ratios are kept in sequence.

52
00:04:30.700 --> 00:04:35.146
The first deep idea is the alternating-current requirement.

53
00:04:35.146 --> 00:04:39.592
If the primary current stays steady, the core flux stops changing, so the secondary no longer experiences ongoing induction.

54
00:04:39.592 --> 00:04:44.037
A.c.

55
00:04:44.037 --> 00:04:48.483
primary means changing flux.

56
00:04:48.483 --> 00:04:52.929
Alternating current keeps the magnetic state of the core changing.

57
00:04:52.929 --> 00:04:57.375
Steady d.c.

58
00:04:57.375 --> 00:05:01.821
means no sustained induction.

59
00:05:01.821 --> 00:05:06.267
A steady state does not keep producing new flux change.

60
00:05:06.267 --> 00:05:10.712
Lesson gain means mechanism becomes clear.

61
00:05:10.712 --> 00:05:15.158
The transformer is built on ongoing change, not just on current being present.

62
00:05:15.158 --> 00:05:19.604
Transformers require changing primary current because they require changing core flux.

63
00:05:19.604 --> 00:05:24.050
Keep this takeaway in view: Transformers require changing primary current because they require changing core flux.

64
00:05:24.050 --> 00:05:28.824
Once the shared-flux mechanism is visible, the turns ratio becomes meaningful.

65
00:05:28.824 --> 00:05:33.598
The side with more turns samples more flux linkage change and therefore gets the larger voltage.

66
00:05:33.598 --> 00:05:38.372
More secondary turns means step-up voltage.

67
00:05:38.372 --> 00:05:43.146
The secondary voltage becomes larger than the primary voltage.

68
00:05:43.146 --> 00:05:47.920
Fewer secondary turns means step-down voltage.

69
00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:52.694
The secondary voltage becomes smaller than the primary voltage.

70
00:05:52.694 --> 00:05:57.468
Quantitative bridge means ratio matters.

71
00:05:57.468 --> 00:06:02.242
Use the turns ratio rather than the labels alone.

72
00:06:02.242 --> 00:06:07.016
Turns ratio is the direct route to voltage ratio.

73
00:06:07.016 --> 00:06:11.790
Keep this takeaway in view: Turns ratio is the direct route to voltage ratio.

74
00:06:11.790 --> 00:06:16.947
The current trade-off is the second key bridge.

75
00:06:16.947 --> 00:06:22.104
If the transformer steps the voltage up ideally, the current must step down so the power does not appear from nowhere.

76
00:06:22.104 --> 00:06:27.261
Step-up case means current falls.

77
00:06:27.261 --> 00:06:32.418
A higher secondary voltage means a lower secondary current for the same ideal power.

78
00:06:32.418 --> 00:06:37.575
Step-down case means current rises.

79
00:06:37.575 --> 00:06:42.732
A lower secondary voltage allows a higher secondary current.

80
00:06:42.732 --> 00:06:47.889
Physical meaning means power stays sensible.

81
00:06:47.889 --> 00:06:53.046
The current ratio keeps the transformer physically consistent.

82
00:06:53.046 --> 00:06:58.203
Use ideal power balance to understand how current changes when voltage changes.

83
00:06:58.203 --> 00:07:03.360
Keep this takeaway in view: Use ideal power balance to understand how current changes when voltage changes.

84
00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:07.223
A structured workflow matters because many errors come from mixing the two ratio jobs together.

85
00:07:07.223 --> 00:07:11.085
First use turns for voltage.

86
00:07:11.085 --> 00:07:14.948
Then use power balance for current.

87
00:07:14.948 --> 00:07:18.811
First calculation means voltage ratio.

88
00:07:18.811 --> 00:07:22.674
Find the unknown voltage from the turns information.

89
00:07:22.674 --> 00:07:26.536
Second calculation means power bridge.

90
00:07:26.536 --> 00:07:30.399
Use ideal power balance only after the voltages are known.

91
00:07:30.399 --> 00:07:34.262
Lesson gain means cleaner solutions.

92
00:07:34.262 --> 00:07:38.125
The ratios stop colliding with each other.

93
00:07:38.125 --> 00:07:41.987
Transformer problems are cleaner when voltage is solved before current.

94
00:07:41.987 --> 00:07:45.850
Keep this takeaway in view: Transformer problems are cleaner when voltage is solved before current.

95
00:07:45.850 --> 00:07:50.137
Use this checkpoint to see whether the turns ratio is being read quantitatively instead of as a label.

96
00:07:50.137 --> 00:07:54.423
Question means five times as many secondary turns.

97
00:07:54.423 --> 00:07:58.710
What should happen to the secondary voltage ideally?

98
00:07:58.710 --> 00:08:02.997
Route means voltage follows turns.

99
00:08:02.997 --> 00:08:07.283
More linked turns means more induced voltage.

100
00:08:07.283 --> 00:08:11.570
Answer check means voltage increases by factor of five.

101
00:08:11.570 --> 00:08:15.857
The transformer is step-up in voltage.

102
00:08:15.857 --> 00:08:20.143
The voltage ratio follows the turns ratio directly.

103
00:08:20.143 --> 00:08:24.430
Keep this takeaway in view: The voltage ratio follows the turns ratio directly.

104
00:08:24.430 --> 00:08:29.582
Carry one route into the worked example.

105
00:08:29.582 --> 00:08:34.733
Start from changing primary current and shared core flux, use the turns ratio to find the missing voltage, then use ideal power balance to get the missing current.

106
00:08:34.733 --> 00:08:39.885
Step 1 means mechanism.

107
00:08:39.885 --> 00:08:45.037
Make sure the changing-flux bridge is clear.

108
00:08:45.037 --> 00:08:50.188
Step 2 means voltage.

109
00:08:50.188 --> 00:08:55.340
Use the turns ratio.

110
00:08:55.340 --> 00:09:00.492
Step 3 means current.

111
00:09:00.492 --> 00:09:05.643
Use ideal power balance.

112
00:09:05.643 --> 00:09:10.795
Keep these relations visible: confirm changing primary current and shared core flux; V_p / V_s = N_p / N_s; and V_p I_p = V_s I_s.

113
00:09:10.795 --> 00:09:15.947
Treat the current step as a second bridge that comes after the voltage step, not as a replacement for it.

114
00:09:15.947 --> 00:09:21.098
The transformer method is strongest when the two ratio jobs are kept in order.

115
00:09:21.098 --> 00:09:26.250
Keep this takeaway in view: The transformer method is strongest when the two ratio jobs are kept in order.
